 ## Evaluation
 
+Muse Spark 1.3, 10 defects (9 planted + 1 real open-source bug) × 3 repeats, run
+`20261027-101729-meta`. Every claimed patch is applied to a fresh copy together with **hidden
+tests** no system has seen; "fixed" means target passes, hidden tests pass, nothing regresses.
+
+| | Baseline A · one-shot | Baseline C · edit-test loop | FORGE-0 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Fixed (hidden-test confirmed) | 28/30 | **30/30** | **30/30** |
+| False fix claims | **2** | 0 | 0 |
+| Regressions | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| Evidence with each fix | none | test results | hypotheses ruled out, 3.9 experiments, flaky-test class, independent verification |
+| Cost per investigation (list price) | $0.012 | $0.013 | $0.071 |
+| Mean time | 23 s | 25 s | 63 s |
+
+The honest reading: with a model this strong, any system that runs the tests and retries matches
+FORGE-0's fix rate on this set at a fifth of the cost; only the one-shot baseline claims fixes that
+are not real (both on D6, the two-cause defect). FORGE-0's difference here is the evidence behind
+each fix, which is what makes it safe to open as a pull request.
+
+Three harder defects (D10–D12) and an open-weights arm were then
+[pre-registered](benchmarks/preregistration/2026-10-27-harder-defects.md) before running:
+
+| Pre-registered arm | One-shot | Edit-test loop | FORGE-0 |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Muse Spark 1.3, D10–D12 × 3 | 9/9 | 9/9 | 9/9 |
+| Muse Spark Open only, all 13 defects × 3 | — | **38/39**, 1 false claim | 30/39, **0** false claims |
+
+The predictions that the harder defects would trip the loop were wrong. On the open model, seven
+of FORGE-0's nine misses were its experiment-design call returning no plan (the model spent its
+whole token budget reasoning); fixing that is the next change, to be measured as a new run.
+Details, the two-model run and threats to validity: [docs/EVALUATION.md](docs/EVALUATION.md).
